# Files NOT uploaded as public Cloudflare assets.
#
# WHY THIS FILE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS
# wrangler.jsonc sets assets.directory to "." — the whole repo root is published.
# .vercelignore used to hold this list, but the Cloudflare migration did not
# carry it over, so dev tooling went public again: /player-health.test.js,
# /seo-ssr.test.js, /asset-perf-check.js and /perf-baseline.json all answered
# 200, and /.env.example served real credentials.
#
# Anything the BROWSER loads must stay out of this list: index.html, the
# moviezone/tv-mode/search-engine/pwa-install bundles, the CSS, sw.js,
# manifest.json, collections-catalog.json, robots.txt, sitemap*.xml, fonts and
# icons. assetsignore-check.js enforces exactly that.

# Directory excludes carry a TRAILING SLASH.
#
# This list started as `**/node_modules` and `**/.git`, and neither one worked:
# verified against production, https://moviezone.dev/node_modules/terser/package.json
# answered 200, so ~13,500 dependency files were being published on every deploy.
# The slash is what makes wrangler prune the directory instead of walking into it.
# Both forms are listed because the root-level one is the case that matters here
# and the `**/` one covers nested copies.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.git/
**/.git/
**/.DS_Store
**/wrangler.jsonc

# Browser test harness pages
*.test.html

# Node test suites
*.test.js

# One-off verification scripts (carousel geometry, OTT ids, SEO SSR, TV perf…)
*-check.js

# Dev-only data: TV perf baseline, asset seal ledger, and the 500 KB sitemap
# catalogue that only the server reads.
perf-baseline.json
asset-versions.json
sitemap-cache.json

# Server-side and tooling code. The browser never requests these; serving them
# just publishes our internals.
server.js
seo-ssr.js
# The OTT chart adapter. Server-side only: server.js requires it and wrangler
# bundles it into worker.js from "main". Serving it as a static file would
# publish the JustWatch query shapes and the package mapping for no benefit —
# the browser only ever talks to /api/ott/charts.
ott-charts.js
# wrangler bundles worker.js from "main"; it must not ALSO be served as a static
# file, or /worker.js hands out our routing, KV key scheme and cron guard.
worker.js
instrument.js
asset-seal.js
ecosystem.config.js
datadogRUM-init.js
scripts/

# Environment files and dependency manifests must never be published.
.env
.env.*
package.json
package-lock.json

# Config for other platforms
vercel.json
netlify.toml
_perf-block.txt

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SCRATCH ARTEFACTS FROM THE BROWSER TEST HARNESSES
#
# cwv-check.js, cat-tabs.browser.test.js, rum-gate.browser.test.js and the other
# CDP harnesses launch Chrome with a throwaway --user-data-dir next to the repo.
# Those directories got committed, and because assets.directory is ".", they were
# then PUBLISHED: 1,134 files / 53 MB, including Chrome's own
# Default/Network/Cookies, Default/Login Data, Default/Web Data and
# Default/History. Verified against production - /cp7.tmp/Default/Login%20Data
# answered 200.
#
# Excluding them here stops the publishing. It does NOT remove them from git
# history, which is a separate decision (see the note in the handover): anything
# that has been publicly served has to be treated as disclosed.
#
# Patterned rather than named so the next harness run cannot reintroduce the
# problem under a new number.
*.tmp/
**/*.tmp/
cp*.tmp/
shots.tmp/

# Scratch images from the banner/OG tooling. Never referenced by any page, and
# banner-desktop.tmp.png alone is 536 KB of deploy weight.
*.tmp.png
*.tmp.js

# Unminified sources. `npm run build` produces the .min siblings and index.html
# references only those (asset-perf-check.js asserts the bare forms are absent),
# so these are 1.2 MB of deploy weight that no browser ever requests.
#
# Only files that HAVE a .min sibling belong here. universes.css deliberately
# does not: it has no minified build and index.html loads it directly, so
# excluding it 404s the Cinematic Universe rail's styling. assetsignore-check.js
# caught exactly that.
moviezone.js
moviezone.css
tv-mode.js
tv-mode.css
search-engine.js
pwa-install.js
